    "description": "ProcurementCore manages purchase requests, approvals, and receipt status, but it must obtain supplier status from AcmeERP through Acme's SOAP SDK and proprietary database views. The external system calls an accepted purchase PO-1042, an in-progress PO-1043, and a rejected PO-1044; Acme reports rejection reasons using financial codes, while ProcurementCore distinguishes supplier rejection from internal approval failure. Amounts arrive in minor units, quantities use case-sensitive UOM codes, and an ERP outage may surface as a timeout, SOAP fault, or HTTP error. Acme also changes SDK field names during planned upgrades. ProcurementCore must not expose Acme types, status codes, units, or failure meanings in its domain or REST contracts. Create a Procurement-owned boundary component that calls Acme, translates requests, results, units, and failures into ProcurementCore concepts, and prevents Acme-specific details from entering purchase aggregates or other callers.",
    "decision_statement": "Should ProcurementCore place Acme-specific calls, data, and failure semantics behind its own translation boundary?",
    "notes": "anti-corruption-layer fits the Procurement-owned boundary translating Acme's incompatible statuses, units, and failures; converter is the field-mapping near miss and message-translator supports wire-format conversion."
  },
